Below is a **clean, security-focused comparison guide for nftables FORWARD chain rules** (IPv4 + IPv6) showing:

* Basic → Advanced commands
* ❌ Unsecure version
* ✅ Secure alternative
* Differences explained
* Real production best practice

Works on:

* Red Hat Enterprise Linux
* Ubuntu
* nftables

---

# 1. What is FORWARD chain?

FORWARD = traffic passing THROUGH your system (router/firewall role)

Example:

```
LAN → Linux Server → Internet
Internet → Linux Server → LAN
```

---

# 2. Secure Base (BEST PRACTICE START)

## ✅ Secure (Recommended baseline)

```bash id="s1"
nft add chain inet filter forward '{ type filter hook forward priority 0 ; policy drop ; }'
```

### Meaning:

* Default = DROP everything
* Only allowed traffic passes

---

## ❌ Unsecure alternative

```bash id="u1"
nft add chain inet filter forward '{ type filter hook forward priority 0 ; policy accept ; }'
```

### Meaning:

* Everything is allowed through system

---

## Difference

| Type            | Behavior             | Security    |
| --------------- | -------------------- | ----------- |
| DROP (secure)   | Block all by default | 🔒 Safe     |
| ACCEPT (unsafe) | Allow all traffic    | ❌ Dangerous |

---

# 3. Allow Established Connections

## ✅ Secure

```bash id="s2"
nft add rule inet filter forward ct state established,related accept
```

### Meaning:

* Allows reply traffic only
* Required for NAT/router

---

## ❌ Unsecure alternative

```bash id="u2"
# No state tracking used
```

### Problem:

* New connections may bypass rules or behave unpredictably

---

## Difference

| Feature        | Secure | Unsecure |
| -------------- | ------ | -------- |
| State tracking | Yes    | No       |
| Security       | High   | Low      |

---

# 4. LAN → WAN Forwarding

## ✅ Secure (interface-based control)

```bash id="s3"
nft add rule inet filter forward iif "eth1" oif "eth0" accept
```

### Meaning:

* Only LAN (eth1) → Internet (eth0)

---

## ❌ Unsecure alternative

```bash id="u3"
nft add rule inet filter forward ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 accept
```

### Problem:

* Any interface spoofing allowed
* Less control

---

## Difference

| Feature  | Secure          | Unsecure |
| -------- | --------------- | -------- |
| Control  | Interface-based | IP-based |
| Security | Strong          | Medium   |

---

# 5. Block WAN → LAN

## ✅ Secure

```bash id="s4"
nft add rule inet filter forward iif "eth0" oif "eth1" drop
```

### Meaning:

* Internet cannot access LAN

---

## ❌ Unsecure alternative

```bash id="u4"
# No blocking rule added
```

### Problem:

* LAN exposed to internet

---

## Difference

| Feature    | Secure | Unsecure |
| ---------- | ------ | -------- |
| Protection | Yes    | No       |

---

# 6. Allow ALL Forwarding

## ❌ Unsecure (VERY DANGEROUS)

```bash id="u5"
nft add rule inet filter forward accept
```

### Meaning:

* System acts like open router

---

## ✅ Secure alternative

```bash id="s5"
nft add rule inet filter forward iif "eth1" oif "eth0" accept
```

### Meaning:

* Only controlled routing allowed

---

## Difference

| Feature        | Secure | Unsecure  |
| -------------- | ------ | --------- |
| Access control | Yes    | No        |
| Risk level     | Low    | Very high |

---

# 7. Block ALL Forwarding

## ❌ Unsecure mistake

```bash id="u6"
nft add rule inet filter forward drop
```

### Problem:

* Breaks routing instantly if rules not added first

---

## ✅ Secure version (safe design)

```bash id="s6"
nft add chain inet filter forward '{ type filter hook forward priority 0 ; policy drop ; }'
```

### Why better:

* Built-in default security
* No accidental bypass

---

## Difference

| Feature   | Secure       | Unsecure        |
| --------- | ------------ | --------------- |
| Design    | Policy-based | Rule-based      |
| Stability | High         | Risk of lockout |

---

# 8. IPv6 Forwarding (Critical comparison)

## ❌ Unsecure

```bash id="u7"
nft add rule inet filter forward ip6 nexthdr icmpv6 drop
```

### Problem:

* Breaks IPv6 networking completely

---

## ✅ Secure

```bash id="s7"
nft add rule inet filter forward ip6 nexthdr icmpv6 accept
```

### Meaning:

* Allows required IPv6 network functions

---

## Difference

| Feature        | Secure | Unsecure |
| -------------- | ------ | -------- |
| IPv6 stability | Works  | Broken   |

---

# 9. Selective Subnet Forwarding

## ✅ Secure

```bash id="s8"
nft add rule inet filter forward ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 iif "eth1" oif "eth0" accept
```

### Meaning:

* Only trusted LAN subnet allowed

---

## ❌ Unsecure

```bash id="u8"
nft add rule inet filter forward accept
```

### Problem:

* Any network can route traffic

---

## Difference

| Feature  | Secure            | Unsecure |
